[{:db/id 17592186155409 :answer-field/correct 17592186155414 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155420 :answer-field/correct 17592186155421 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155415 :answer-field/correct 17592186155416 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337177"] :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1/1be23322a705239834ee3034ce66e7e2d3abe104c3198062f03111fa44fff1d8" :answer/id #uuid "bf91ad93-f4fc-57c1-b550-94972f6af7f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1/58e66e13dc98313f1c71e73a32f04479429e770bf3852598ea62e9f4fbb454e6" :answer/id #uuid "9a37f52f-19f5-5348-8b18-bbb6d57b341b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1/90244fea567263d29a122e974f426c797e64ce018b292ff9ad249e079209d91d" :answer/id #uuid "11676e06-4095-5f0d-92c7-faa115376ebb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1/1c87915a6c57b766bbb06d9fd3b05c238a68caca6be59893391c9af122458273" :answer/id #uuid "7ac95a8a-ee51-585b-9a6e-f0b5bc80ae5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1/0dfd7211371cc7d8403c054fcef962b61d8a8feaf3d34d77257754f397cd581b" :answer/id #uuid "e9c5dcb2-cebd-565b-a414-f1f6198f9f45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb87f899-5dbf-5dd3-a094-edb96e87079c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337376/field-1/1be23322a705239834ee3034ce66e7e2d3abe104c3198062f03111fa44fff1d8" :answer-field/choices ["q-337376/field-1/1be23322a705239834ee3034ce66e7e2d3abe104c319