0a46412674bc2ec817f412c343d901a6974e0364fa9f046b5b8a13eb9b3" "q-337376/field-3/2ebc133599183e5fdb5b6248a8ea00bdd0440ff336252ee383591a8699a758ca" "q-337376/field-3/c43b27454cd69f1b6f4b793e211f4f4f6e40314b87f5b1c35a69172a60fcd818" "q-337376/field-3/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337376" :question/id #uuid "a01f9794-773d-5e12-a888-6dc684788a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-337376" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337376/field-1" "q-337376/field-2" "q-337376/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337376"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337399/field-1/58e66e13dc98313f1c71e73a32f04479429e770bf3852598ea62e9f4fbb454e6" :answer/id #uuid "202aaccc-96ae-52e4-9820-fef38221c8df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337399/field-1/0f0bf3e5b4d49d098cc5baf660c6977edc9ec113af6f735d59f497de50943951" :answer/id #uuid "9eafaf0e-0d87-521f-9946-b9d3730e99a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337399/field-1/1be23322a705239834ee3034ce66e7e2d3abe104c3198062f03111fa44fff1d8" :answer/id #uuid "b41e2c24-5887-569b-ac33-8c5e7e9bbfe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337399/field-1/0dfd7211371cc7d8403c054fcef962b61d8a8feaf3d34d77257754f397cd581b" :answer/id #uuid "f1ac3957-5174-57d1-a973-90edce9bd8ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337399/field-1/90244fea567263d29a122e974f426c797e64ce018b292ff9ad249e079209d91d" :answer/id #uuid "fdba04b0-8008-559f-951c-526e8f5121b3" :answer/type :an