}^{5x}+x{e}^{-5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337524/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a637610-9f3e-5843-ae2c-b744dd3e59d7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-337524/field-3/7d5ea06e4c88875f4f26e08342056352aa0afa85f6ad8a8849fd8a2430eb5d45" :answer-field/choices ["q-337524/field-3/f813c4312d1e81574b5e5a03aa49ff2457dd97a230dde016facc08b9a02c41b2" "q-337524/field-3/7d5ea06e4c88875f4f26e08342056352aa0afa85f6ad8a8849fd8a2430eb5d45" "q-337524/field-3/6370ea0b73730951071d29e5922799e43008386658043910acad1dcc28e21840" "q-337524/field-3/ae13eadda41a77da6ec641698b4c0b3ffe2633f3a8445e0a39d75313468a739f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337524" :question/id #uuid "54ef0c16-1df4-5060-9377-447e0ea12bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337524" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =20\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =20{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x=\\frac{{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =20{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡5x \\\\  & =20{e}^{-5x}(\\frac{{e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}}{2}) \\\\  & =10{e}^{-5x}({e}^{5x}-{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =10-10{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =10-10{e}^{-10x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 10-10{e}^{-10x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =10x+{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =10x{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337524/field-1" "q-337524/field-2" "q-337524/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337524"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
